  "answer": "I'll look through the product special-price path and how the storefront decides whether a discount is active.The discount check looks wrong when an end date is present. I'll trace which admin create path writes those dates and which storefront path reads them.The end date is saved onto the start-date field, and the price check then compares the start date to itself, so a dated special never becomes active. I'll correct both.A special price that included an end date never reached the storefront as a discount. The admin product mapper stored that end date in the special start-date field, and the catalogue price calculation then compared the start date with itself, so the window could never be active. The end date is now saved on the end-date field, and the price check treats the special as active only while today is after the start (when one is set) and before the end.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-core -am test -Dtest=ProductPriceUtilsDiscountTest -DfailIfNoTests=false\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"PersistableProductAvailabilityMapper date mapping was not executed; no admin or storefront API call was made\"], \"missing\": [\"an existing test or fixture that creates a product through the admin REST API and reads it from the storefront API\"]}",
